course: history
lesson: 34
title: The Second World War
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In the 1930s a huge economic crisis, the Great Depression, left millions without jobs, and in some countries frightened, angry people turned to dictators who promised strength and blamed others.
explain: In Germany Adolf Hitler and his Nazi Party took power in 1933, ended free elections and free speech, persecuted Jews and others, and set out to conquer other countries.
explain: In 1939 Germany invaded Poland and a war began that spread across the whole world; British children were sent to the countryside with a gas mask and a name label, and Ukraine was one of the places worst hit of all.
explain: The Nazis murdered about six million Jews, and many others, only because of who they were; this is called the Holocaust, and we remember it so that such hatred never happens again.
explain: Together the Allies were stronger: the war ended in 1945, and countries founded the United Nations, like a playground agreeing on rules so the biggest fight never starts again.
fact: In the 1930s the Great Depression left millions jobless, and in some countries people turned to dictators who used fear and blame to take power.
fact: Adolf Hitler and the Nazi Party took power in Germany in 1933, ended free elections and free speech, and persecuted Jews and others.
fact: In September 1939 Germany invaded Poland, and Britain and France declared war; the Second World War lasted until 1945 and was the largest war in history.
fact: In 1940 Germany conquered much of Europe, but Britain, led by Winston Churchill, fought on, and the Royal Air Force won the Battle of Britain in the skies.
fact: In the Blitz German planes bombed British cities, and many children were evacuated to the countryside with a gas mask and a label to live with strangers.
fact: Ukraine was one of the places worst hit: fighting went back and forth across it, cities were ruined, and millions of Ukrainians died as soldiers and as civilians.
fact: The Nazis murdered about six million Jews, as well as many Roma, disabled people and others, only because of who they were; this is called the Holocaust.
fact: Anne Frank, a Jewish girl, wrote a diary while hiding from the Nazis in Amsterdam, and it tells one girl's story to millions of readers.
fact: The Soviet Union was attacked by Germany in June 1941, and the USA joined the war after Japan attacked Pearl Harbor in December 1941.
fact: The Allies, including Britain, the Soviet Union, the USA and China, fought against Germany, Italy and Japan, and soldiers came from all over the world, including India and Africa.
fact: On 6 June 1944, D-Day, Allied troops landed on the beaches of Normandy in France, the largest landing from the sea in history, which began the freeing of western Europe.
fact: The war ended in Europe in May 1945 and in Asia in August 1945, after the USA dropped atomic bombs on two Japanese cities, Hiroshima and Nagasaki; each single bomb destroyed most of a city.
fact: In 1945 countries founded the United Nations to keep peace and settle quarrels by talking.
exercise: What was the Great Depression? | A huge economic crisis that left millions jobless | A long war in Europe that lasted the whole of the 1930s | A great flood that drowned farms all over America | A deep hole dug in Germany
exercise: When did Hitler and the Nazis take power in Germany? | 1933 | 1914 | 1945 | 1066
exercise: What did the Nazis end in Germany? | Free elections and free speech | Football matches and all music on the radio | Farming in the countryside | All school holidays for children
exercise: Which country did Germany invade in 1939 to start the war? | Poland | Britain | The USA | Australia
exercise: Who led Britain when it fought on in 1940? | Winston Churchill | Queen Elizabeth the First | President Abraham Lincoln | Sir Isaac Newton the scientist
exercise: What was the Battle of Britain? | A battle in the skies won by the Royal Air Force | A great sea battle against the Spanish Armada near Plymouth | A battle fought by knights on horses | A tank battle in France
exercise: What happened to many British city children during the Blitz? | They were evacuated to the countryside | They were all trained to fly fighter planes for the RAF | They sailed to Australia on their own | Nothing changed for them
exercise: What did evacuee children carry? | A gas mask and a name label | A wooden sword and a shield for playing soldiers | A rifle and a soldier's helmet | A big map of the bomb shelters
exercise: How did the war affect Ukraine? | Fighting crossed it, cities were ruined and millions died | Ukraine stayed far from the fighting, like a quiet island | It was the one peaceful country in Europe | Only a few villages were touched
exercise: What was the Holocaust? | The Nazi murder of about six million Jews and many others | A famous battle at sea between British and German ships in 1940 | A new kind of fast aeroplane | The peace treaty that ended the war
exercise: Who was Anne Frank? | A Jewish girl who wrote a diary while hiding | A British pilot who won the Battle of Britain in 1940 | A French queen in a castle | An American nurse
exercise: Why did the USA join the war in 1941? | Japan attacked Pearl Harbor | Germany sent its army to invade Canada | Britain begged them to come and play football | The Moon landing failed
exercise: Which countries were on the Allied side? | Britain, the Soviet Union, the USA and China | Germany, Italy and Japan | Only Switzerland | Only France
exercise: What happened on D-Day, 6 June 1944? | Allied troops landed on the beaches of Normandy | The war began when Germany invaded Poland | The Berlin Wall fell and Germany was joined together again | Japan surrendered
exercise: How did the war end in Asia in August 1945? | Japan surrendered after atomic bombs fell on two cities | Japan won and took over all of China and India | Everyone just went home in May | A great storm sank all the Japanese ships near the coast
exercise: What was set up in 1945 to keep peace? | The United Nations | The modern Olympic Games | The European Union and the euro | The Holy Roman Empire
test: kid in yt comments says ww2 was just britain v germany. fix him | It was fought across the world by many countries | He is right, only two countries | It was only in America | It was only at sea
test: Why could fear and hard times in the 1930s help dictators? | Frightened people were drawn to leaders who promised strength and blamed others | Dictators gave every family a big bag of money each week | People voted for war on purpose because they liked fighting | Hard times always make people kinder to each other
test: If Britain had given up in 1940, what might have happened? | Germany would have controlled nearly all of western Europe | The war would have ended happily and everyone would be friends | Britain would have conquered Germany soon after | Nothing at all would have changed
test: Why do millions of people still read Anne Frank's diary? | It shows one real girl's life and helps us understand the Holocaust | It is a funny adventure story about pirates on a desert island | It is a big book of maps of Europe | It was written by a general after the war
test: Why is it wrong to say Ukraine was not important in the Second World War? | It was one of the worst hit places, with millions of Ukrainians killed | Ukraine did not exist then, so no Ukrainians took part in the war | It stayed neutral like Switzerland | It is right, nothing much happened there
test: An evacuee aged eight arrives at a farm to live with strangers. How would she most likely feel at first? | Homesick and scared, but safer from the bombs | Happy that she would never have to see her family again | Bored, because nothing at all had changed | Excited to join the army
test: Why does the world remember the Holocaust every year? | So that such hatred never happens again | To celebrate a great victory over Germany in battle | Because it is a happy holiday with presents | It does not; people have forgotten all about it
test: D-Day needed ships, planes and soldiers from many countries at once. Why so big? | Landing on a guarded coast needed a huge force | They were going on holiday | It was a race | Only one boat was used
test: why did they bother making the UN after the war | To help countries settle quarrels by talking instead of fighting | To organise a world football league with teams from every country | To build more and bigger bombs | To choose one king to rule the world
test: Why did the atomic bomb make people more afraid of another big war? | One bomb could destroy a whole city | It was much louder than any bomb before | It was made of pure gold | It could only hit ships at sea
exam: Identify the event which in September nineteen hundred and thirty-nine precipitated the Second World War. | The German invasion of Poland | The attack upon Pearl Harbor | The landing in Normandy | The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Holocaust? | The murder by the Nazi regime of approximately six million Jews and many others | A naval engagement in the Pacific Ocean | An economic crisis of the nineteen-thirties | The evacuation of British children
exam: Ascertain the date upon which Allied forces landed upon the beaches of Normandy. | The sixth of June, nineteen hundred and forty-four | The eleventh of November, nineteen hundred and eighteen | The first of September, nineteen hundred and thirty-nine | The fourth of July, seventeen hundred and seventy-six
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the organisation founded in nineteen hundred and forty-five for the preservation of peace. | The United Nations | The League of Nations | The European Union | The Hanseatic League
